===Etymology 1===
From {{suffix|en|draw|er|t1=to move by pulling|id2=agent noun}}; compare {{cog|fr|tiroir}}. Attested from the 16th century.<ref>drawer, n.{{sup|2}}, {{R:OED}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An open-topped [[box]] that can be slid in and out of the [[cabinet]] that contains it, used for storing clothing or other articles.
# {{lb|en|GUI}} A side panel containing supplementary content.
# {{attributive form of|en|drawers|cap=1}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Corset and Underwear Review|volume=12|year=1918|page=74|passage=The step-ins are of two sorts; the knicker combination with open bloomer and chemise top; and circular '''drawer''' style, with chemise top and gussets at back and front that give extra width at the bottom.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Underwear & Hosiery Review|volume=5|year=1922|page=67|column=2|passage=Customers in the underwear department at MacDougal-Southwick’s, on the other hand, have evinced quite an interest in the French '''drawer''' style, with binding in contrasting color and the separate vests also bound in the contrasting color.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=directed by Amos Parrish|title=What’s In Fashion: Underwear For Men|journal=w:The Pittsburgh Press|volume=XLVI|issue=305|location=Pittsburgh, Pa.|date=26 April 1930|page=ten|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/146459160/|column=4|passage=Today men of every age are turning to the athletic pull-over shirt and '''drawer''' type of underwear.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Hand-Picked Bargains–''from a Store full of them!''|journal=w:Democrat and Chronicle|location=Rochester, N.Y.|date=4 May 1935|page=3|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/135209258/|column=3|passage=Men’s Balbriggan Shirts and Drawers / 50<sup>c</sup> ea. / For the man who wants the shirt-and-'''drawer''' type of underwear but, naturally, wants lightweight now.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Nell DuVall|title=Domestic Technology: A Chronology of Developments|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=w:G. K. Hall & Co.|year=1988|page=366|isbn=9780816189137|passage=Leloir described ''les calecons'', '''drawer'''-type undergarment worn by French ladies ({{smallcaps|wc}}).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gian Singh Sandhu|title=Who Are the Sikhs?: An Exploration of the Beliefs, Practices, & Traditions of the Sikh People|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=Archway Publishing|year=2023|isbn=978-1-6657-3952-8|passage=The Sikh code of conduct does not restrict how Sikhs dress, except that they must wear ''kashera'' ('''drawer'''-type, cotton tie-able underwear) and a turban.}}

